1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,478
Time to put the last five lectures together into the one document you'll actually

2
00:00:05,478 --> 00:00:10,670
run before every batch ships, the same document that caught the swelling claim and

3
00:00:10,670 --> 00:00:13,266
the licence-tier gap on Harlan's own set.

4
00:00:13,266 --> 00:00:14,532
Nothing here is new.

5
00:00:14,532 --> 00:00:16,115
Claims, from Section 6.2.

6
00:00:16,115 --> 00:00:17,191
Rights, from 6.4.

7
00:00:17,191 --> 00:00:18,520
Disclosure, from 6.3.

8
00:00:18,520 --> 00:00:21,686
Accuracy, the taxonomy's fourth question from 6.1.

9
00:00:21,686 --> 00:00:23,775
Owners and checkpoints, from 6.5.

10
00:00:23,775 --> 00:00:28,840
This lecture's only job is assembly, the same way the static and video workshops

11
00:00:28,840 --> 00:00:32,196
turned earlier sections into one repeatable template.

12
00:00:32,196 --> 00:00:37,261
Why does assembly deserve its own lecture rather than a slide at the end of 6.5?

13
00:00:37,261 --> 00:00:41,883
Because five separate lectures' worth of checks, however good each one is

14
00:00:41,883 --> 00:00:47,011
individually, don't run themselves as a coherent pass unless someone deliberately

15
00:00:47,011 --> 00:00:51,063
puts them in one place, in order, with the checkpoints attached.

16
00:00:51,063 --> 00:00:56,191
That's genuinely different work from teaching each check on its own, and it's the

17
00:00:56,191 --> 00:00:58,217
work this lecture actually does.

18
00:00:58,217 --> 00:01:03,092
Think of this the way Section 2.6 turned five earlier lectures into one brief

19
00:01:03,092 --> 00:01:08,094
template, or the way Section 5.4 turned three platform lectures into one export

20
00:01:08,094 --> 00:01:08,537
matrix.

21
00:01:08,537 --> 00:01:13,855
The pattern repeats across this entire course on purpose: teach each piece properly,

22
00:01:13,855 --> 00:01:18,540
on its own, then hand you the one document that runs all of them together.

23
00:01:18,540 --> 00:01:23,732
That's what actually survives into your real workflow, not the individual lectures

24
00:01:23,732 --> 00:01:24,428
themselves.

25
00:01:24,978 --> 00:01:29,893
Here's the whole checklist, four questions, four owners, run at the checkpoints

26
00:01:29,893 --> 00:01:34,932
Section 6.5 already laid out: claims and rights before generation, accuracy right

27
00:01:34,932 --> 00:01:36,674
after, disclosure at upload.

28
00:01:36,674 --> 00:01:38,353
Save this exactly as it is.

29
00:01:38,353 --> 00:01:43,330
The value isn't in the wording, it's in having one document that answers "did we

30
00:01:43,330 --> 00:01:48,307
check everything" without anyone having to reconstruct five lectures from memory

31
00:01:48,307 --> 00:01:49,800
under deadline pressure.

32
00:01:49,800 --> 00:01:52,289
Notice, too, how short this actually is.

33
00:01:52,289 --> 00:01:57,079
Four lines, four owners, no paragraph of explanation attached to any of them,

34
00:01:57,079 --> 00:02:02,180
because the explanation already happened, properly, across five separate lectures.

35
00:02:02,180 --> 00:02:07,157
A checklist that's short enough to read in thirty seconds is one people actually

36
00:02:07,157 --> 00:02:07,468
read.

37
00:02:07,468 --> 00:02:12,383
A checklist that reads like a policy document is one people learn to skip past.

38
00:02:12,383 --> 00:02:14,809
Notice the shape of each question, too.

39
00:02:14,809 --> 00:02:19,973
All four are yes-or-no, the same discipline Section 6.1 insisted on from the start.

40
00:02:19,973 --> 00:02:24,016
"Could each claim survive being said out loud" has a real answer.

41
00:02:24,016 --> 00:02:29,056
A vaguer version, "does this feel honest," doesn't, and a checklist full of vague

42
00:02:29,056 --> 00:02:32,913
questions gets skipped exactly as fast as no checklist at all.

43
00:02:33,463 --> 00:02:35,702
Notice this isn't a hypothetical run.

44
00:02:35,702 --> 00:02:40,666
Two of the four checks actually caught something real on Harlan's own account, the

45
00:02:40,666 --> 00:02:44,056
two you've already seen in full in Sections 6.2 and 6.4.

46
00:02:44,056 --> 00:02:47,991
This checklist isn't a formality that happens to pass every time.

47
00:02:47,991 --> 00:02:52,954
It's the thing that actually caught two genuine problems before either one spent a

48
00:02:52,954 --> 00:02:53,378
dollar.

49
00:02:53,378 --> 00:02:56,344
Notice, too, which checks passed cleanly and why.

50
00:02:56,344 --> 00:03:01,066
Accuracy passed because Section 3's colour and text fixes were already applied

51
00:03:01,066 --> 00:03:03,608
before this batch ever reached pre-flight.

52
00:03:03,608 --> 00:03:04,577
That's not luck.

53
00:03:04,577 --> 00:03:09,177
It's what happens when the earlier sections' discipline is already built in:

54
00:03:09,177 --> 00:03:12,809
pre-flight catches what's left over, not everything at once.

55
00:03:12,809 --> 00:03:17,107
That's actually the healthiest way to think about this checklist's job.

56
00:03:17,107 --> 00:03:20,436
It isn't the only line of defence in this whole course.

57
00:03:20,436 --> 00:03:25,218
It's the last one, catching whatever slipped through every earlier check, brand

58
00:03:25,218 --> 00:03:30,242
control in Section 3, licence tiers in Section 4, the claims discipline you've been

59
00:03:30,242 --> 00:03:34,782
building since Section 2.3's hook taxonomy first taught you to be specific.

60
00:03:34,782 --> 00:03:39,746
Four checks here, not forty, because forty would mean the earlier sections weren't

61
00:03:39,746 --> 00:03:40,715
doing their job.

62
00:03:41,265 --> 00:03:44,942
One honest expectation before you run this on your own batch.

63
00:03:44,942 --> 00:03:48,317
Passing zero for four on the first try would be unusual.

64
00:03:48,317 --> 00:03:53,381
Failing one or two, the way Harlan's batch did, is the normal result of a real check

65
00:03:53,381 --> 00:03:54,767
actually doing its job.

66
00:03:54,767 --> 00:03:59,408
Measure success the same way Section 3.6 and 4.6 already taught you to: not a

67
00:03:59,408 --> 00:04:04,411
perfect first pass, but every real problem caught here, before Section seven's test

68
00:04:04,411 --> 00:04:06,521
finds it the expensive way instead.

69
00:04:06,521 --> 00:04:11,283
Ask yourself, honestly, what a zero-for-four result on your own first run would

70
00:04:11,283 --> 00:04:12,127
actually mean.

71
00:04:12,127 --> 00:04:16,889
Either your batch is genuinely cleaner than Harlan's, which is possible, or the

72
00:04:16,889 --> 00:04:21,169
checklist isn't being run with real scrutiny, which is far more common.

73
00:04:21,169 --> 00:04:25,810
If nothing ever fails, go back and check whether you're actually testing each

74
00:04:25,810 --> 00:04:29,728
question against the batch, or just glancing at it and moving on.

75
00:04:30,278 --> 00:04:32,249
One lecture left in this section.

76
00:04:32,249 --> 00:04:36,906
Then Section seven takes a batch that's already passed every one of these four

77
00:04:36,906 --> 00:04:41,743
checks and finally puts it in front of a real test, with a budget and a kill rule

78
00:04:41,743 --> 00:04:42,281
attached.

79
00:04:42,281 --> 00:04:46,819
Before that, run this exact checklist on your own three video ads and twenty

80
00:04:46,819 --> 00:04:50,282
statics, the same way you just watched it run on Harlan's.

81
00:04:50,282 --> 00:04:54,940
Name an owner for each check, even if every owner is you, and note what fails.

82
00:04:54,940 --> 00:04:59,896
Whatever it catches now is cheaper than whatever it would have cost you to find out

83
00:04:59,896 --> 00:05:03,061
